WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.899
Film Courage: Is it crucial to have multiple
projects going at once?

00:00:02.899 --> 00:00:04.610
Mark Sanderson, Screenwriter and Author: Yes.

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:05.610
Film Courage: Why?

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:10.900
Mark Sanderson: Because like salmon going
upstream, a few will get up there.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:14.639
I mean you have to…one script is not going
to do it as I always say.

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:17.360
One script eventually does open something.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.690
Like for me, my spec opened the door for assignment
work with the company, you know.

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:29.120
I continued on with that but if you just focus
your energy on one thing, it’s a lot…the

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:35.960
odds that…you have to be constantly…then
that was my problem I’ll share with you.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:41.210
When I sold my spec, I didn’t have a follow-up
and that really was a problem because I thought

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:43.050
“Here I am!”

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:51.370
And there was no follow-up for awhile and
that was my mistake because I wasn’t thinking

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:52.370
in those terms.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:58.300
You know, it was early on in my pursuit, I
just graduated from college and was not thinking

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:03.870
in that structured like…”Okay, what’s
my game plan?” and I knew a friend who went

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:08.380
to film school and he directed a movie that
he wrote but he didn’t have a follow-up

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:12.490
and they spent so much time on this movie
and it was a feature and he didn’t sell

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:18.759
it, it didn’t get picked up by anyone, he
paid money to show it in a theater and they…”Okay,

00:01:18.759 --> 00:01:20.319
what else do you have?”

00:01:20.319 --> 00:01:21.529
“Nothing.”

00:01:21.529 --> 00:01:27.490
So you constantly (as I say in the book),
you have to have ideas, you have to have log

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:31.450
lines that you’re working on and then you
also have to have some treatments or one-sheets

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:35.700
which are a lot easier to do than write a
complete screenplay and then you also have

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.670
to have your screenplay that you’re working
on.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:43.179
But constantly when you go into a meeting
with your spec that gets you the meeting,

00:01:43.179 --> 00:01:47.049
they’re not buying that spec they just want
to meet you and say “Oh, I can see your

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:49.280
ability” and then they say “What else
you have?”

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.969
And you don’t want that to be “Well, I’m
working on something and it’s going to take

00:01:52.969 --> 00:01:54.099
me six months.”

00:01:54.099 --> 00:01:55.969
I’m not going to see you for six months?

00:01:55.969 --> 00:01:58.259
Or You’re not going to be on my radar for
six months?

00:01:58.259 --> 00:02:00.929
You want to say “I have this other thing.”

00:02:00.929 --> 00:02:03.979
You don’t want to bring a suitcase full
of scripts but you want to say “I have this

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:04.979
other thing.”

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:05.979
“Oh?

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:07.770
Well after the meeting, send it over.”

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:08.770
BAM!

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:09.770
You know what I mean?

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:14.350
You have to have multiple projects in the
marketplace, I think at all times because

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:19.739
not all of them and maybe not one of them,
but that’s what you’re up against is to

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:25.739
be…I don’t want to say a machine but you
know you can’t be precious with “Oh, I’m

00:02:25.739 --> 00:02:27.950
only working on one thing for five years.”

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:33.090
And if you have the luxury of doing that,
that’s great but you better have a day job

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.370
that pays.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.269
I didn’t want to wait tables the rest of
my life.

00:02:38.269 --> 00:02:44.000
So what motivated me to get out was to continuing
to write and create.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.340
So yeah, I think it takes more than one project.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:50.459
Film Courage: I know you do a lot of speaking
events, is that right?

00:02:50.459 --> 00:02:51.459
Mark Sanderson: I do.

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:55.790
Film Courage: So when you have writers approach
you either afterward or even if you see them

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:58.189
at a mixer or whatever.

00:02:58.189 --> 00:03:01.980
You know when people ask you for advice and
you give it and you can see that they are

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.189
disengaging because they don’t want to hear
the answer?

00:03:04.189 --> 00:03:05.189
Mark Sanderson: Yes.

00:03:05.189 --> 00:03:08.140
Film Courage: What are some of those points
when you’re talking and they’re saying

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:10.500
“How do I break into the industry?”

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.810
Mark Sanderson: Because their experience will
be different than mine which it will be but

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:17.569
their experience will be like “Oh its not
going to happen to me.”

00:03:17.569 --> 00:03:21.319
And like I said multiple projects.

00:03:21.319 --> 00:03:27.549
And so when people do that I say I just I
don’t want to expend any more time bestowing

00:03:27.549 --> 00:03:32.540
any sort of advice because what’s the point.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:35.040
Example: I did a favor for a friend.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.859
Oh my friend wrote this TV pilot.

00:03:36.859 --> 00:03:38.610
Okay, I’ll meet with him.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:50.249
Now I consult in my off times I have a consulting
business but I only do it when I’m not working

00:03:50.249 --> 00:03:54.180
because the real jobs are the assignment jobs.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:58.969
So in the downtime I open it up and say shop’s
open.

00:03:58.969 --> 00:04:01.560
So I thought I’ll do this as a freebie,
as a favor.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.829
And a lot of times it stings you and bites
you in the you-know-where.

00:04:05.829 --> 00:04:10.341
So I went to meet with this guy at Starbucks
and he brings in a giant screenplay.

00:04:10.341 --> 00:04:11.341
And I say “What is this?”

00:04:11.341 --> 00:04:12.341
And he says “It’s a TV pilot.”

00:04:12.341 --> 00:04:13.610
And I go “OKay.”

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:18.930
And he starts dropping names of people that
I know that are going to give him an office

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:19.930
in the thing.

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:22.150
And I’m like “They’re going to give
you an office?

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:23.520
What about me?”

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.560
And so I say okay I’ll read it.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:31.949
So I went home and there were (I’m not kidding
you) ten issues on every page from typos,

00:04:31.949 --> 00:04:38.160
to format to whatever you want and so we met
again and I was really doing my friend a favor

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:47.860
and he had an excuse for every one and wouldn’t
listen and he said “Well, I’m going to

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:49.113
chop it in half and make it a thing.”

00:04:49.113 --> 00:04:50.770
And I’m like “Okay, interesting.”

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:55.060
And still went on and on dropping these names
about some of the people I know are some how

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:59.220
going to bring him in and give him a desk
and an office and I was just like “I can

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:00.730
only do so much.

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:01.730
Good luck to you.”

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:02.830
And maybe I’m wrong?

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:05.880
I doubt it but okay.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.630
People who don’t want to listen it obviously
shows their ignorance.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:14.620
And it’s not…I’m not trying to be malicious
I’m just trying to say “Hey, I wish someone

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:16.810
had done that to me.”

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:20.660
Then it would save me a lot of time and time
is what keep clicking away.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:25.680
When you make mistakes why spend all this
time making mistakes when you can do it right?

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:27.180
Or go down this path?

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.320
Everyone has to learn, hopefully they do.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:35.660
But yes I’ve experienced that where people
just tune off and it’s like “Huh?

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:36.660
Okay?”

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:40.990
Film Courage: And is that around the time
when you say it’s going to take several

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:45.250
scripts, always have multiple things so if
somebody wants to see one?

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:46.250
Mark Sanderson: Yeah, that’s why.

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:48.210
I went to talk at a high school.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:53.580
My friend is a teacher to their film class
and one of the kids raises his hand and goes

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:56.099
“Have you written any big movies?”

00:05:56.099 --> 00:06:00.370
Phew…how long do we have on this?

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:04.569
So we went through the thing and you could
just see all of this.

00:06:04.569 --> 00:06:12.551
They’re not going to…but the few film
nerds which I was one growing up, after it

00:06:12.551 --> 00:06:16.229
was over they came up (three or four came
up) and said “Oh!”

00:06:16.229 --> 00:06:18.500
And they wanted to look at a script and talk
to me directly.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:22.680
Everybody else couldn’t wait to leave and
I just thought “Huh?

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:23.680
Interesting?”

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:26.660
I was making films when I was their age, you
know?

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:28.710
Doing that and they can do whatever.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:30.139
Maybe they weren’t interested, that’s
okay.

00:06:30.139 --> 00:06:34.530
But I always say you’ll be humbled.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:38.889
If you’re not humble now, the craft and
the business will humble you eventually where

00:06:38.889 --> 00:06:40.229
you say “Oh, my one script.”

00:06:40.229 --> 00:06:46.699
And then you learn that after 35 screenplays
either you’re still learning which should

00:06:46.699 --> 00:06:50.889
be and it takes a lot to get one.

00:06:50.889 --> 00:06:55.280
That’s why the multiple projects thing is
important, to have multiple projects going

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:00.330
at each time and that’s not to say you have
to write two projects but always have something.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:06.220
You finish one, you’re working on another,
don’t leave these long gaps of nothing and

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:07.250
you’re out there.

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:12.220
And my spec that I sold it took seven years
but that wasn’t my only thing I had.

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:14.720
I wasn’t running around town with this one
spec.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:17.910
I continued on doing other things.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:19.860
I was in a sketch comedy show.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:26.129
I was doing proactive things at the same time
but the only problem is that once it’s sold

00:07:26.129 --> 00:07:30.220
I didn’t have a specific spec like in the
same genre where they could say “What else

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:31.250
can I look at?”

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:32.910
Where things weren’t ready.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:36.939
You know that’s important too to have those
other things ready because like I said if

00:07:36.939 --> 00:07:40.069
you have that meeting and they say “Oh send
over something,” you don’t want it to

00:07:40.069 --> 00:07:45.030
be six months later, you want it to be immediate,
immediately.

